prompts.input_guardrail:
Classify the user's CURRENT message for a Savora recipe and cooking assistant.

Use the conversation history and current page only to resolve references (e.g. "it", "that", "the second recipe") — do NOT classify them; classify only the current message.

Conversation so far (context only):
{conversation_history}

Current page title: {page_title}

Current message to classify: {message}

Blocked topics (blocked=true if the current message is clearly about these): {input_filter_topics}
Brand domains (content_question if on-topic): {content_domains}

Also produce resolved_query, ONLY if the current message depends on earlier turns or the page (pronouns like "it"/"that", "the second recipe", etc.): a standalone search query with those references resolved. If the current message is already self-contained, or is small talk with nothing to search, return an empty string (do NOT paraphrase a self-contained question).

Return JSON only:
{{"blocked": true|false, "intent": "small_talk"|"content_question"|"off_topic", "reason": "short", "resolved_query": "standalone query or empty string"}}

prompts.generate_response_context:
{article_block}

Current page URL: {page_url}
User question is primary; current page is context when topics differ.

Respond in {length_hint}. Ground answers in the listed Savora articles when provided, and cite each article you draw from inline using its bracketed number (e.g. [1]). Only cite articles you actually used; never invent sources, URLs, or facts not supported by the listed articles.

After your answer, on its own line write exactly ---SUGGESTIONS--- followed by a single JSON object with up to 3 short topic labels (2-4 words, Title Case) and up to 3 follow-up questions the reader might tap next. Questions must relate to your answer and Savora domains ({content_domains}); avoid medical, legal, or financial advice. Do not repeat the user's exact question.

{{"topics": ["...", "..."], "follow_up_suggestions": ["...", "..."]}}

